y = -10 + 10 = 0 => not (5,1) or (5,2). At x=6, y = -12 + 10 = -2 => not.\n\nThus none of the lines cover any points in columns 5 or 6. So we need additional lines to cover those points, but we have no lines left. So this assignment fails.\n\nWe could try different pairings: maybe we can pair A_i with points in columns 5 or 6 directly, leaving some B_j uncovered, but we need to cover all points. Let's try to assign each line to include a point from column 3 and maybe also a point from column 5 or 6, and then cover column 4 points with other lines. Since column 3 has 4 points, we need 4 lines. Each line can have at most one point from each column. So we could assign each line to have a point from column 3 and maybe also a point from column 5 or 6, but we need to cover all points in column 4 as well. Since column 4 has 3 points, we can assign them to three of the four lines, leaving one line without a column 4 point. Similarly, column 5 has 2 points, assign to two lines, column 6 has 1 point assign to one line. So each line could have up to 4 points (one from each column). So we need to find 4 lines each passing through a distinct point in column 3, and also passing through some points in columns 4,5,6 as needed. This is a combinatorial geometry problem. The solution claims impossible for k≥4. Let's try to see if we can find a construction for n=6, k=4. Perhaps we can find lines that go through (3,1) and (4,2) and (5,3) and (6,4). But (5,3) and (6,4) are not in T_n because 5+3=8 >7, 6+4=10 >7. So not allowed. So lines cannot go through points beyond the triangle. So maybe we can use lines that go through (3,4) and (4,2) and (5,0) (not in T_n). So not helpful.\n\nMaybe we can use lines that go through (3,1) and (5,2) and (6,1). Let's compute slope between (3,1) and (5,2): slope = (2-1)/(5-3) = 1/2 = 0.5, sunny. Equation: y - 1 = 0.5 (x - 3) => y = 0.5 x - 0.5. At x=6, y = 3 - 0.5 = 2.5, not (6,1). So not.\n\nMaybe line through (3,2) and (5,1): slope = (1-2)/(5-3) = -1/