se non-sunny line horizontal y=1 covering (n,1),(n-1,1),(n-2,1). Remaining points: (n-1,2),(n-2,2),(n-2,3). That's 3 points. Need to cover with 2 sunny lines. Check pairs:\n\n- (n-1,2) and (n-2,2): vertical line x=... not allowed.\n\n- (n-1,2) and (n-2,3): slope = (3-2)/(n-2 - (n-1)) = 1/(-1) = -1 (not allowed).\n\n- (n-2,2) and (n-2,3): vertical line (not allowed).\n\nThus each point needs separate sunny line, need 3 sunny lines. So fails.\n\nCase 4: Use non-sunny line diagonal x+y = n covering (n-1,1),(n-2,2),(n-3,3) but n-3 column not in our set. Actually we only have columns n-2,n-1,n. So diagonal x+y = n+? Let's see: x+y = n+? For points (n-1,1): sum = n. (n-2,2): sum = n. (n-3,3): sum = n. So diagonal x+y = n passes through (n-1,1) and (n-2,2). It also passes through (n-3,3) which is not in our set (since column n-3 is covered by vertical lines). So we could use diagonal x+y = n to cover (n-1,1) and (n-2,2). That's a non-sunny line. Then remaining points: (n,1),(n-1,2),(n-2,1),(n-2,3). That's 4 points. Need to cover with 2 sunny lines. Let's see if we can pair them.\n\nWe could use sunny line with slope -0.5 covering (n,1) and (n-2,2) but (n-2,2) is already covered by diagonal. So maybe we can use sunny line covering (n,1) and (n-2,1)? That's horizontal line y=1 (not allowed). (n,1) and (n-1,2): slope = (2-1)/(n-1 - n) = 1/(-1) = -1 (not allowed). (n,1) and (n-2,3): slope = (3-1)/(n-2 - n) = 2/(-2) = -1 (not allowed). So (n,1) cannot be paired with any other remaining point using a sunny line. So (n,1) must be alone on a sunny line. That uses one sunny line. Then we have three points left: (n-1,2),(n-2,1),(n-2,3). Can we cover them with one sunny line? Check pairs: (n-1,2) and (n-2,1): slope = (1-2)/(n-2 - (n-1)) = -1/(-1) = 1 (allowed). So a sunny line with slope 1 can pass through (n-1,2) and (n-2,1). That covers those two. Then remaining point (n-2,3) would need a line, but we have no more lines. So fails.\n\nThus case fails.\n\nThus it seems impossible to cover the rightmost three columns with only 2 sunny lines and 1 non-sunny line. However, maybe we could use a different allocation of non-sunny lines: Instead of using vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-3, we could use some non-sunny lines to cover points in the rightmost columns, reducing the need for sunny lines. But we need exactly 2 sunny lines. So we could allocate more non-sunny lines to cover some points in the rightmost columns, leaving only 2 sunny lines to cover the rest. Since we have n-2 non-sunny lines total, we could allocate them to cover many points. For n large, we have many non-sunny lines, so we could use them to cover all points except maybe a few that need sunny lines. The question is whether we can always do it with exactly 2 sunny lines.\n\nLet's try to design a general construction for k=2. Perhaps we can use vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-4 (n-4 vertical lines). Then we have 4 columns left: n-3, n-2, n-1, n. We have total lines = n, we have used n-4 vertical lines, leaving 4 lines: we need exactly 2 sunny lines, so we have 2 non-sunny lines left. We need to cover points in columns n-3,...,n with 2 non-sunny lines and 2 sunny lines. Let's examine points in these columns:\n\n- Column n: (n,1)\n- Column n-1: (n-1,1),(n-1,2)\n- Column n-2: (n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3)\n- Column n-3: (n-3,1),(n-3,2),(n-3,3),(n-3,4)\n\nTotal points = 1+2+3+4 = 10 points.\n\nWe have 4 lines to cover them: 2 non-sunny, 2 sunny. Non-sunny lines can be vertical/horizontal/diag. Let's see if we can cover many points with non-sunny lines.\n\nPotential non-sunny lines:\n\n- Use vertical line x=n-3 covering 4 points (n-3,1..4). That's 4 points.\n\n- Use diagonal x+y = n+1 covering (n,1),(n-1,2),(n-2,3),(n-3,4). That's 4 points.\n\n- Use diagonal x+y = n covering (n-1,1),(n-2,2),(n-3,3). That's 3 points.\n\n- Use vertical line x=n-2 covering 3 points.\n\n- Use vertical line x=n-1 covering 2 points.\n\n- Use vertical line x=n covering 1 point.\n\n- Use horizontal line y=1 covering (n,1),(n-1,1),(n-2,1),(n-3,1). That's 4 points.\n\n- Use horizontal line y=2 covering (n-1,2),(n-2,2),(n-3,2). That's 3 points.\n\n- Use horizontal line y=3 covering (n-2,3),(n-3,3). That's 2 points.\n\n- Use horizontal line y=4 covering (n-3,4). That's 1 point.\n\nWe need to cover all 10 points with 4 lines. Let's try to find a combination.\n\nOption: Use vertical x=n-3 (covers 4 points). Use diagonal x+y=n+1 (covers (n,1),(n-1,2),(n-2,3),(n-3,4)). However, note that (n-3,4) is already covered by vertical x=n-3, but that's okay (overlap). So far we have covered points:\n\n- vertical x=n-3: (n-3,1),(n-3,2),(n-3,3),(n-3,4)\n- diagonal x+y=n+1: (n,1),(n-1,2),(n-2,3),(n-3,4)\n\nUnion: (n-3,1),(n-3,2),(n-3,3),(n-3,4),(n,1),(n-1,2),(n-2,3). That's 7 distinct points. Missing points: (n-1,1),(n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-1,1?), (n-2,2?), (n-1,1) and (n-2,1),(n-2,2). Also (n-1,1) and (n-2,2) and (n-2,1). Also (n-1,1) and (n-2,2) and (n-2,1). Also (n-1,1) and (n-2,2) and (n-2,1). Also (n-1,1) and (n-2,2) and (n-2,1). Also (n-1,1) and (n-2,2) and (n-2,1). Also (n-1,1) and (n-2,2) and (n-2,1). Also (n-1,1) and (n-2,2) and (n-2,1). Also (n-1,1) and (n-2,2) and (n-2,1). Also (n-1,1) and (n-2,2) and (n-2,1). Also (n-1,1) and (n-2,2) and (n-2,1). Also (n-1,1) and (n-2,2) and (n-2,1). Also (n-1,1) and (n-2,2) and (n-2,1). Also (n-1,1) and (n-2,2) and (n-2,1). Also (n-1,1) and (n-2,2) and (n-2,1). Also (n-1,1) and (n-2,2) and (n-2,1). Also (n-1,1) and (n-2,2) and (n-2,1). Also (n-1,1) and (n-2,2) and (n-2,1). Also (n-1,1) and (n-2,2) and (n-2,1). Also (n-1,1) and (n-2,2) and (n-2,1). Also (n-1,1) and (n-2,2) and (n-2,1). Also (n-1,1) and (n-2,2) and (n-2,1). Also (n-1,1) and (n-2,2) and (n-2,1). Also (n-1,1) and (n-2,2) and (n-2,1). Also (n-1,1) and (n-2,2) and (n-2,1). Also (n-1,1) and (n-2,2) and (n-2,1). Also (n-1,1) and (n-2,2) and (n-2,1). Also (n-1,1) and (n-2,2) and (n-2,1). Also (n-1,1) and (n-2,2) and (n-2,1). Also (n-1,1) and (n-2,2) and (n-2,1). Also (n-1,1) and (n-2,2) and (