istinct points covered by (n - k) non-sunny lines is at most (n - k) * n - (some overlaps). Overlaps can be at most (n - k) - 1 (if each pair intersects at a distinct point). Actually each pair of non-sunny lines intersect at a point (if they are not parallel). For vertical and horizontal lines, they intersect at a unique point. For vertical and diagonal, they intersect at a point. For horizontal and diagonal, they intersect at a point. So if we use lines from different families, each pair may intersect at a distinct point. So the total number of distinct points covered by (n - k) non-sunny lines is at most (n - k) * n - C where C is number of intersections counted multiple times. The maximum distinct points is achieved when we minimize overlaps, i.e., choose lines from different families that intersect at distinct points. For (n - k) lines, the maximum number of intersections among them is at most choose((n - k), 2) (if all pairs intersect at distinct points). So distinct points ≤ (n - k) * n - choose((n - k), 2). But we need to consider that each line has at most n points, but some lines have fewer points (vertical lines near right side have fewer points). So the bound may be lower.\n\nNevertheless, the uncovered points must be covered by sunny lines. Each sunny line can cover at most some number of points, maybe up to n (if we can find a line with many points). But the maximum number of points a sunny line can cover is limited by the geometry. For slope 1 lines, we can have up to floor((n+1)/2) points? Actually line y = x + c (slope 1) intersects the triangle. For c = 0, line y = x passes through points (1,1),(2,2),... up to (⌊(n+1)/2⌋,⌊(n+1)/2⌋) because we need a+b ≤ n+1 => 2a ≤ n+1 => a ≤ floor((n+1)/2). So the line y = x contains floor((n+1)/2) points. For n=6, floor(7/2)=3 points: (1,1),(2,2),(3,3). So a sunny line can cover up to floor((n+1)/2) points. Similarly, lines with other slopes may have similar maximum lengths.\n\nThus, the total number of points th